      "id": "summary",
      "proposition": {
        "predicate": "summary",
        "object": "<p><strong>Alison Smith </strong>says food bans do not improve compliance, they create a false sense of security in patients</p><p>As the mother of a child with anaphylactic allergies, I am sympathetic to the terror that Lucy Pasha-Robinson describes (<a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2026/aug/27/food-allergies-flying-planes-banned-peanuts\">Food allergies when flying are no joke. That’s why nuts should be banned on planes, 27 August</a>). Indeed, last summer I spent six hours compulsively cleaning on a demonstrably filthy flight. Flight crew handed out wipes to passengers “to clean your tray tables”, then proceeded to serve packets of cashews (my child’s anaphylactic nut) with drinks. My&nbsp;nerves were shredded by the time we arrived in Chicago.</p><p>However, I am firmly against the banning of nuts from the skies. Food bans do not improve compliance, and they create a false sense of security in patients.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/society/2026/sep/02/banning-nuts-from-the-skies-would-not-keep-people-with-allergies-safe\">Continue reading...</a>"
      },
